'''iter†, titerer†.''' Old spellings of ''titter''<sup>1</sup>, ''titterer''.

'''it-for-tat''' (tit′fộr-tat′), ''n.'' See ''tit''<sup>4</sup>.

'''ith†''' (tith), ''adv.'' [A var. of ''tite''<sup>1</sup>, < ME. ''tit'', ''tid'', quickly: see ''tide''<sup>1</sup>.] Same as ''tite''<sup>1</sup>.
{{smaller block|Of a good stirring strain too, she goes ''tith.''
{{right|''Fletcher,'' Loyal Subject, iii. 4.}}}}

'''ithable''' (tī′ᴛ̵ʜạ-bl), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [Also ''titheable''; < ''tithe'' + ''-able.''] '''I.''' ''a.'' 1. Subject to the payment of tithes, as property; capable of being tithed.
{{smaller block|It is not to be expected from the nature of these general commentaries that I should particularly specify what things are ''tithable'' and what not, the time when, or the manner and proportion in which, tithes are usually due.
{{right|''Blackstone,'' Com., II. iii.}}}}
2†. Assessable for tithes, or for the payment of any tax to a parish, as a person.
{{smaller block|They [Virginians] call all negroes above sixteen years of age ''tithable,'' be they male or female, and all white men of the same age.{{right|''Beverley,'' Virginia, iv. ¶ 18.}}}}

'''II.'''† ''n.'' A person by or for whom tithes or parish taxes were payable.
{{smaller block|Their parishes are accounted large or small, in proportion to the number of ''tithables'' contained in them, and not according to the extent of land. ''Beverley,'' Virginia, iv. ¶ 33.}}

'''tithe<sup>1</sup>''' (tīᴛ̵ʜ), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [Formerly also ''tythe''; < ME. ''tithe'', ''tythe'', ''tethe'', < AS. ''teótha'' for *''teóntha'', < ''teón'', ''tién'', ''tȳne'', ten: see ''ten'', ''tenth.''] '''I.'''† ''a.'' Tenth.
{{smaller block|Every ''tithe'' soul, 'mongst many thousand.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. and C., ii. 2. 19.}}}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' 1. A tenth; the tenth part of anything; hence, any indefinitely small part.
{{smaller block|I have searched . . . man by man, boy by boy; . . . the ''tithe'' of a hair was never lost in my house before.}}

