'''tithe'''<sup>1</sup> (tīᴛ̵ʜ), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''tithed'', ppr. ''tithing''. [Formerly also ''tythe''; < ME. ''tithen'', ''tythen'', ''tethen'', < AS. ''teóthian'', tithe, < ''teótha'', tithe, tenth: see ''tithe''<sup>1</sup>, ''a''.] I. ''trans.'' 1. To subject to tithes or the payment of a tithe; impose a tithe or tenth of or upon.

{{smaller block|When thou hast made an end of ''tithing'' all the tithes of thine increase.
{{right|Deut. xxvi. 12.}}}}

2. To pay tithes on; give or yield up a tithe of.

{{smaller block|Military spoil, and the prey gotten in war, is also tythable, for Abraham ''tythed'' it to Melchizedek.
{{right|''Spelman'', Tythes, xvi.}}}}

3†. To take or reckon by tenths or tens; take tithe or every tenth of.
